 after: datetime) -> datetime:\n-    moment = after.replace(second=0, microsecond=0) + timedelta(minutes=1)\n-    for _ in range(400 * 24 * 60):\n-        if self._matches(expression, moment):\n-            return moment\n-        moment += timedelta(minutes=1)\n+    \"\"\"Scan day by day, and inside every matching day minute by minute.\"\"\"\n+    parsed = parse(expression)\n+    hours = sorted(parsed.hour.values)\n+    minutes = sorted(parsed.minute.values)\n+    day = after.date()\n+    for offset in range(8 * 366):\n+        current_day = day + timedelta(days=offset)\n+        if not self._day_matches(parsed, current_day):\n+            continue\n+        for hour in hours:\n+            for minute in minutes:\n+                candidate = datetime(\n+                    current_day.year, current_day.month, current_day.day, hour, minute\n+                )\n+                if candidate > after:\n+                    return candidate\n     raise AssertionError(f\"{expression!r} did not fire during the scan\")\n \n def test_matches_the_brute_force_scan(self):\n     after = datetime(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0)\n","additions":20,"deletions":14},"truncated":false},"title":"private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/cron-scheduler/opencode-2/ws/app/tests/test_cronlite.py","time":{"start":1794861220591,"end":1794861220599}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait, in the brute force test, expression \"45 23 31 12 *\" fires once a year (Dec 31 at 23:45) — the brute force scan of 400 days covers it. ✓\n\n\"0 0 29 2 *\" — brute force scan of 400 days from 2026-01-01 wouldn't reach Feb 29 2028! That would fail the brute force test. Let me check: after = 2026-01-01, next fire = 2028-02-29. The brute-force scan of 400 days = until 2027-02-05. So it would raise AssertionError.\n\nI need to handle that. Options: remove \"0 0 29 2 *\" from the brute-force list, or extend the scan range (e.g., 4 years = 2.1M minutes — slow in Python but maybe acceptable... 2.1M iterations × 14 expressions is way too slow).\n\nBetter: use a smarter brute force that skips days when the day doesn't match — but that would replicate the logic. Alternative: brute force over days and hours/minutes: iterate day by day (max ~1500 days = 4 years) and check the day match, then within the day check hours/minutes. That's a semi-brute force but independent of the stepping logic. It reduces iterations to ~1500 per expression, fast.\n\nLet me restructure the brute force:\n- iterate over days starting from the day of `after`\n- for each day, if the day matches, iterate over the 24 hours × 60 minutes (or use sorted lists of allowed hours/minutes but that's reusing the parsed sets — that's fine, it's the parse result, not the stepping logic).\n- return the first candidate strictly after `after`.\n\nThat's a fair independent check of the ordering/stepping logic. Days scanned: up to 8 years = ~2900 days. Fine.\n\nLet me rewrite `_brute_force`:\n\n```python\ndef _brute_force(self, expression, after):\n    parsed = parse(expression)\n    day = after.date()\n    for offset in range(370 * 8):\n        current_day = day + timedelta(days=offset)\n        if not self._day_matches(parsed, current_day):\n            continue\n        for hour in sorted(parsed.hour.values):\n            for minute in sorted(parsed.minute.values):\n                candidate = datetime(current_day.year, current_day.month, current_day.day, hour, minute)\n                if candidate > after.replace(second=0, microsecond=0) and candidate > after:\n                    return candidate\n    raise AssertionError(...)\n```\n\nCareful with the \"strictly after\" comparison: candidate must be > after (the raw `after` with seconds). Since candidates have zero seconds, `candidate > after` works: if after = 12:00:00 and candidate = 12:00:00 → not strictly after → skip. If after = 12:00:30 and candidate = 12:00:00 → 12:00:00 > 12:00:30 is False → skip. ✓ That's exactly \"strictly after after\".\n\nNote: `next_run` ignores seconds of `after`, meaning it truncates. So a fire at 12:00 with after=12:00:30 is NOT after 12:00:30 → next fire. ✓ consistent with `candidate > after`.\n\nAlso for months: the brute force iterates over all days in all months and checks `parsed.month.values` — I need to include the month check in the day matcher.\n\nLet me rewrite the brute force properly. Also \"7 7 7 7 7\" → July 7 at 07:07 → the day-of-week is restricted (7) and DOM restricted (7) → OR semantics → every July 7 plus every Monday in July. Fine.\n\nLet me rewrite the BruteForceTests class.\n\nAlso, the \"test_matches_the_brute_force_scan_from_other_moments\" with after = datetime(2028, 2, 28, 23, 59) and expression \"0 0 29 2 *\" → 2028-02-29 00:00 ✓ (within a few days).\n\nLet me rewrite the file section.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e610c2bb001ciz0FNSJOLythg","sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","messageID":"msg_1e6109455001Z1Y2fEN1EaOj8z"}}
