dout/cron-scheduler/opencode-2/ws/app/tests/test_cronlite.py","time":{"start":1794861258400,"end":1794861258459}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"All 47 tests pass. Now let me strengthen the test suite with a few more important checks:\n\n1. A test for the 8-year horizon explicitly. How can I test the boundary? I need an expression whose next firing is more than 8 years away but exists. Consider dom=`*` (unrestricted), dow restricted, month restricted: e.g. `0 0 * 2 1` → Mondays in February. Feb Mondays are frequent → fires within a year.\n\nWhat about `0 0 29 2 *`? Feb 29 occurs every 4 years → within 8 years always.\n\nTo exceed 8 years, I'd need something like \"Feb 29 that is also a Monday\" — but OR semantics prevent AND. Hmm, unless... dom is restricted and dow is restricted → OR. So no AND possible. Therefore, the only \"sparse\" expressions are of the form: restricted dom with month (day-of-month + month) or restricted dow + month, or dom + month combos.\n\nWith dom restricted + month restricted: e.g. dom=29, month=2 → every 4 years. dom=30, month=2 → never. dom=31, month={2,4,6,9,11} → never (those months have 30 days except Feb).\n\nMax gap for a real date: Feb 29 → 4 years (8 years when crossing a non-leap century, e.g. 2096 → 2104, but that's >8 years away!). So `0 0 29 2 *` after 2096-03-01 → next Feb 29 = 2104-02-29, which is ~7.99 years later. Let's compute: from 2096-03-01, 8 years → 2104-03-01. 2104-02-29 < 2104-03-01 → so it IS within 8 years. Hmm, the limit is computed from `current` (after + 1 minute), so limit = 2104-03-01 00:01 → 2104-02-29 00:00 <= limit → fires. OK.\n\nWhat about after = 2096-03-02 → next Feb 29 = 2104-02-29; limit = 2104-03-02 → still within.\n\nThe 8-year window always covers the next Feb 29 (since gaps are 4 years, or 8 years across a non-leap century: 2096 → 2104 is exactly 8 years). After 2096-03-01, next Feb 29 = 2104-02-29 which is 7 years 364 days later < 8 years. So it always fits.\n\nSo the 8-year limit only matters for never-firing expressions. Fine — the important behavior is that a never-firing expression raises ValueError. I test that. I could also test that the error message mentions \"8 years\". Let me add a check that the ValueError message mentions 8 years.\n\n2. Also test that `next_run` for an expression with hour values spanning, e.g. `0 23 * * *` after 23:00 → next day 23:00 ✓ (covered by brute force).\n\n3. Test the month field with a list, e.g. `0 0 1 1,7 *`.\n\n4. Test `0 0 * * 0` on a Sunday boundary.\n\nLet me add a couple more targeted tests:\n- error message mentions 8 years\n- DOM/DOW OR semantics across a month boundary (e.g. \"0 0 31 * 1\" — 31st of month OR Monday).\n- a test that the result of next_run can be fed back into next_run to get the next one.\n\nLet me also double check a subtle case in the algorithm: when the current day matches but the hour set has no hour >= current.hour, we move to the next day at 00:00 and re-check the day match. ✓\n\nAnother subtle case: when current matches day+hour but the minute set has no minute > current.minute, we look for the next hour in the set > current.hour; if found, set hour and minute=0; but we don't re-check that the minute 0 is in the minute set! Bug? If minute set = {30} and hour set = {0, 12}: current = 2026-01-01 00:05, hour 0 ok, minute 5 not in {30} → next minute in set > 5 → 30 → current = 00:30 ✓. But consider current = 00:45 (minute 45 not in {30}, and 45 > 30) → next minute > 45 → none → next hour > 0 → 12 → current = 12:00 → but minute 0 is NOT in the minute set {30}! We return 12:00 which is wrong — it should be 12:30.\n\nLet me verify with the brute force test — the expression \"0,30 0,12 * * *\" was in the brute-force list and the test passed... Let me check: the brute force iterates over days, then hours (sorted: 0, 12), then minutes (sorted: 0, 30) — for each candidate it checks `candidate > after` and returns the first. The brute force iterates hours in ascending order and minutes in ascending order within the hour → returns the earliest candidate ✓. But the ordering of the brute force loop is: for hour in hours: for minute in minutes: → candidates are 00:00, 00:30, 12:00, 12:30 → the first candidate > after is returned. That's correct ordering.\n\nSo why did the brute force test pass for \"0,30 0,12 * * *\"? Because in the specific `after` moments used, the bug wasn't triggered? after = datetime(2026,1,1,0,0): current = 00:01 → day ok, hour 0 ok, minute 1 not in {0,30} → next minute > 1 → 30 → 00:30 ✓. The bug triggers only when the current minute is > 30 and the next hour exists, e.g. after = 00:45 → current = 00:46 → minute not in set → next minute > 46 → none → next hour 12 → current = 12:00 → minute 0 IS in the set {0,30} → no bug! Because the minute set contains 0.\n\nThe bug needs a minute set that doesn't contain 0, e.g. minute={30} only. Example: after = 00:45 with expression \"30 0,12 * * *\" → current = 00:46 → minute 46 not in {30} → no next minute → next hour = 12 → current = 12:00 → returns 12:00, but the correct answer is 12:30.\n\nLet me confirm and fix. The fix: when jumping to the next hour, set minute to the minimum of the minute set (not necessarily 0), i.e. `current.replace(hour=next_hour, minute=self._minutes[0])`. Since we know the minute set is non-empty, using minutes[0] is correct: the earliest firing minute in that hour.\n\nSimilarly, when jumping to the next day at 00:00, that's fine because the day/hour/minute checks are re-evaluated afterwards.\n\nAlso in the \"hour not in hours\" branch: `current.replace(hour=next_hour, minute=0)` → should be minute = minutes[0] as well? Let's see: in that branch, we set the hour to the next allowed hour; the minute should be the earliest allowed minute, i.e. minutes[0]. If minutes[0] != 0, then using 0 would be wrong (e.g. minute set {30}: current = 05:00, hour 5 not allowed, next hour 12 → current = 12:00 → then the minute check: 0 not in {30} → next minute > 0 → 30 → 12:30 ✓). Actually that path still recovers correctly because the minute check happens after. But in the branch where I jump to the next hour *after* failing the minute check, I `continue` the loop → the loop re-checks month, day, hour (now the new hour is allowed ✓), then minute: current.minute = 0, which is not in {30} → next minute > 0 → 30 → correct!\n\nWait, let me re-trace: the bug case is current = 00:46, minute set {30}, hour set {0,12}.\n- day matches ✓, hour 0 ∈ hours ✓, minute 46 ∉ {30} → next_minute = None → next_hour = 12 (12 > 0) → current = current.replace(hour=12, minute=0) → 12:00 → continue.\n- Loop iteration: month ✓, day ✓ (same day), hour 12 ∈ hours ✓, minute 0 ∉ {30} → next_minute = _next_above({30}, 0) = 30 → current = 12:30 ✓ → return 12:30.\n\nHmm! That's correct. So where's the bug? Let me re-read my code:\n\n```python\n            if current.minute not in self._expression.minute.values:\n                next_minute = self._next_above(self._minutes, current.minute)\n                if next_minute is not None:\n                    current = current.replace(minute=next_minute)\n                    continue\n                next_hour = self._next_above(self._hours, current.hour)\n                if next_hour is not None:\n                    current = current.replace(hour=next_hour, minute=0)\n                    continue\n                current = _start_of_next_day(current)\n                continue\n```\n\nYes — after setting the hour it `continue`s, so the minute check re-runs and fixes the minute. So no bug. Good — my earlier concern was unfounded because the loop re-checks.\n\nBut wait: there's still an issue with `current.replace(hour=next_hour, minute=0)` when current.hour > next_hour? No: next_hour > current.hour always.\n\nOK so the algorithm is self-correcting. But to be safe and explicit, using minutes[0] would skip an iteration; not needed.\n\nLet me still add a test with minute set not containing 0 to cover this: e.g. expression \"30 0,12 * * *\" with after = 00:45 → expect 12:30.\n\nLet me verify with the brute force. Also let me add these expressions to the brute-force list: \"30 0,12 * * *\", \"5 3,15 * * *\", \"0 0,12 1 * *\".\n\nLet me also double check the hour-not-in-hours branch: current = 05:00 with hours {0,12} → next_hour = 12 → current = 12:00 → minute check → if minute set {30} → 12:30 ✓.\n\nNow let me also consider the case where the day matches but we're at hour 23 and the next allowed hour is 0 (next day) — `_next_above` returns None → `_start_of_next_day` → correct.\n\nAnother potential issue: after `_start_of_next_day`, the new day might not match (e.g. dom=13) → the loop handles it ✓.\n\nLet me add the extra tests and also a randomized/exhaustive cross-check with more expressions and moments.\n\nLet me update the test file with:\n- more brute-force expressions (minute sets without 0)\n- more \"after\" moments\n- an 8-year error message assertion\n- a test that next_run(next_run(...)) is the following firing\n\nLet me edit.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e61155d90010fEKoBq3HgP4Ad","sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","messageID":"msg_1e6110b9b001ewPWy0I0CEPzsb"}}
