{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794861304710,"sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","part":{"id":"prt_1e6120806001NtJrqxa5DGZeBW","messageID":"msg_1e611eb02001qmayT5htI8gJJk","sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794861304835,"sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_a17eb86c821f4ccfa62075a8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# cronlite\n\nA tiny, dependency free cron expression calculator for Python 3.11+.\n\n`cronlite` answers one question: **when does this cron expression fire next?**\nIt ships as a small library and as a command line tool, and uses nothing but\nthe Python standard library.\n\n## Install\n\nThe package lives in the project root, so no installation is needed:\n\n```console\n$ python -m cronlite \"*/15 * * * *\"\n```\n\nFor a regular installation (which also provides a `cronlite` console script):\n\n```console\n$ pip install .\n```\n\n## Library usage\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom cronlite import next_run\n\nnext_run(\"0 9 * * 1-5\", datetime(2026, 1, 3, 12, 0))\n# datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 5, 9, 0)\n```\n\n`next_run(expression: str, after: datetime) -> datetime`\n\n* Returns the first firing time **strictly after** `after`.\n* Datetimes are naive (no time zone). The seconds and microseconds of `after`\n  are ignored, and the returned datetime always has them set to zero.\n* Raises `ValueError` when the expression is invalid, or when the expression\n  does not fire within eight years of `after`.\n\n## Command line usage\n\n```console\n$ python -m cronlite \"EXPRESSION\" [--after YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM] [--count N]\n```\n\nPrints the next `N` firing times (default `1`), one per line, formatted as\n`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM`. The times are computed after `--after`, which defaults to\nthe current time.\n\n```console\n$ python -m cronlite \"0 9 * * 1-5\" --after 2026-01-03T12:00 --count 3\n2026-01-05T09:00\n2026-01-06T09:00\n2026-01-07T09:00\n```\n\nAn invalid expression or an invalid `--after` value prints a message to\nstandard error and exits with status `2`.\n\n## Expression syntax\n\nFive whitespace separated fields:\n\n| Field            | Range  | Notes                        |\n| ---------------- | ------ | ---------------------------- |\n| minute           | 0-59   |                              |\n| hour             | 0-23   |                              |\n| day of month     | 1-31   |                              |\n| month            | 1-12   |                              |\n| day of week      | 0-6    | `0` is Sunday               |\n\nEach field is a comma separated list of parts. A part is one of:\n\n| Part     | Meaning                                                |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `*`      | every value of the field                               |\n| `N`      | the single value `N`                                   |\n| `A-B`    | the range `A` to `B`, both ends inclusive              |\n| `*/S`    | every `S`-th value of the field, starting at its lowest value |\n| `A-B/S`  | every `S`-th value of `A-B`, starting at `A`           |\n\nExamples: `*`, `0`, `1-5`, `*/15`, `9-17/2`, `0,15,30,45`.\n\nAnything else is rejected: an unknown number of fields, a value outside the\nrange of its field, a range whose start is after its end, a step of `0`, or a\nstray character. Note that only `*/S` and `A-B/S` are supported — a bare\n`N/S` step is not part of the grammar and is rejected.\n\n### Day of month and day of week\n\nThe two fields combine the way classic cron does:\n\n* When **both** fields are restricted (neither is a plain `*`), a day matches\n  if **either** field matches it.\n* When **one** field is a plain `*`, only the **other** field is considered.\n* When both fields are `*`, every day matches.\n\nSo `0 0 13 * 5` fires on the 13th of every month *and* on every Friday, while\n`0 0 13 * *` only fires on the 13th and `0 0 * * 5` only fires on Fridays.\n\n### Dates that never occur\n\nAn expression may be perfectly valid and still never fire, for example\n`0 0 30 2 *` (there is no 30th of February). Such an expression is accepted by\nthe parser; `next_run` gives up with a `ValueError` when nothing fires within\neight years.\n\n## Project layout\n\n```\ncronlite/\n├── __init__.py      public API: next_run(), parse()\n├── __main__.py      the \"python -m cronlite\" command\n├── parser.py        expression grammar and validation\n└── schedule.py      the search for the next firing time\ntests/\n├── test_cronlite.py\n└── test_cli.py\n```\n\n## Tests\n\n```console\n$ python -m unittest discover\n```\n\nThe suite covers the grammar, the day of month / day of week combination, the\ncommand line interface, and cross-checks the fast search against a naive\nday-by-day scan.\n","filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/cron-scheduler/opencode-2/ws/app/README.md"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/cron-scheduler/opencode-2/ws/app/README.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/cron-scheduler/opencode-2/ws/app/README.md","time":{"start":1794861304692,"end":1794861304752}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1e6120832001IZ0CpiEpTnKiSy","sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","messageID":"msg_1e611eb02001qmayT5htI8gJJk"}}
