{"type":"text","timestamp":1794861411277,"sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","part":{"id":"prt_1e613a5a9001aZM1WzUBIrJ02L","messageID":"msg_1e613a2c7001iwo2Pr7szBJyIK","sessionID":"ses_e19f1290fffeOm13DSw4yBRd3L","type":"text","text":"Done. Built `cronlite/` at the project root (stdlib only, Python 3.11+):\n\n**Library** — `from cronlite import next_run`\n- `cronlite/parser.py:81` — strict grammar: `*`, `N`, `A-B`, `*/S`, `A-B/S`; rejects out-of-range values, descending ranges, step `0`, stray chars, and bare `N/S` (not in the spec's grammar).\n- `cronlite/schedule.py:83` — field-by-field search (month → day → hour → minute) instead of minute-by-minute scanning; classic DOM/DOW combination (both restricted → OR; one `*` → the other wins); `ValueError` after the 8-year horizon.\n\n**Command** — `python -m cronlite \"EXPRESSION\" [--after YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM] [--count N]`\n- Prints `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM` lines; invalid expression or `--after` → stderr message, exit 2.\n\n**Verification**\n- 49 unittest tests (`python -m unittest discover`) plus a pytest run — all pass; ruff/flake8/mypy clean.\n- The suite cross-checks the fast search against a naive day-by-day scan; I additionally fuzzed 24,000 random expressions against an independent brute force (all matched).\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec's part list omits `N/S`, so `5/2` is rejected as invalid (documented in the README) — real cron accepts it, so say the word if you want it allowed.","time":{"start":1794861409705,"end":1794861411188}}}
